Subject: [PATCH] memsys5 blocks and lookaside slots as Sublet lifetimes, block bounds, realloc renews
Upstream: sqlite 3.22.0 amalgamation
Upstream-SHA256: 7fc92e19a3af61f3961b83447a91f1e1dec06646e343037dfd96a397167700b2 (sqlite3.c)
Applies-To: sqlite3-capstone.c, the copy ports/sqlite/repro322/build-virtual.py
  stages (sqlite3.c with fts5-azarg-patch.py and fts3-doclist-null-patch.py),
  with patch -F0 -p1

The block port (2026-10-26). Every block memsys5 hands out is a child
lifetime of its pool, bounded to the whole power-of-two block, as stock
memsys5 hands it out and reports it; sqlite3_free revokes it, so every
copy of the caller's pointer dies with it. Every successful realloc ends
the old allocation and returns a new lifetime, also when the block stays:
realloc's contract (C11 7.22.3.5, SQLite's sqlite3_realloc) frees the old
allocation whether or not the address changes. The lookaside does the
same for its slots. Placement, sizes, rounding and heap geometry are
stock memsys5's; the only extra operation is a CRENEW per realloc that
keeps its block.

# Three instructions do the work. CDERIVE makes a child capability of a
# non-linear parent, bounded to a sub-range; CREVOKE(object) ends the object
# and everything derived inside it, and faults when the object is not live;
# CRENEW(object) ends it the same way and begins a new lifetime in its
# place, and returns a reference with the operand's cursor and bounds.
# CREVOKE and CRENEW take the object alone: they name no parent and need no
# right.
#
# memsys5Init derives the pool from the configured heap, as its child, with
# stock geometry (nByte/(szAtom+1) atoms). memsys5MallocUnsafe returns a
# child of the pool bounded to the block, memsys5FreeUnsafe revokes it
# before the buddy code runs, and memsys5Shutdown revokes the pool. Split
# and merge are unchanged: they only move indices, and memsys5 reaches every
# block, the in-band free-list links included, through the pool. A block is
# a power-of-two number of atoms at an index that is a multiple of its size,
# and the pool base carries the heap's granule alignment
# (repro322_common.h), so every child is representable without a pad.
#
# realloc. A realloc that fits the block reaches memsys5Realloc's in-place
# branch, which calls memsys5Renew: CRENEW on the caller's pointer. The old
# lifetime ends and a new one takes its place under the pool. The pointer
# returned has the cursor and bounds of the caller's, and those are the
# block's, because memsys5 hands out every block at its first byte and
# bounded to the whole block. SQLite's own shortcut in sqlite3Realloc (same
# rounded size: return the old pointer without calling the allocator) is
# removed, so that case reaches memsys5Renew as well. sqlite3DbRealloc's
# lookaside branch (the slot still fits) renews the slot the same way
# (lookasideRenew). A realloc that moves is unchanged: allocate, copy, then
# free the old block, which revokes it. A failed realloc leaves the old
# allocation and its lifetime as they were. Growth into a block's unused
# bytes without a realloc, which SQLite does after asking sqlite3MallocSize,
# is no lifetime boundary and changes nothing.
#
# The ablation: -DSUBLET_REALLOC_KEEPS_LIFETIME returns the old child from
# every realloc that keeps its block and restores SQLite's shortcut, so a
# stale pointer across an in-place realloc goes undetected, as on a
# mechanism that sees only frees.
#
# What it gives up: an overrun inside a block's power-of-two rounding is not
# caught, as on PoisonCap's memsys5. A block carries the heap's data rights.
#
# setupLookaside derives the lookaside's generation from the buffer, a
# memsys5 block or a buffer the application supplied alike.
# sqlite3DbMallocRawNN returns a child of the generation bounded to the slot
# (lookasideIssue); sqlite3DbFreeNN revokes it before pushing the slot on the
# free list through pStart (lookasideRelease), and trashes it, under
# SQLITE_DEBUG, through pStart as well. A new setup and the close of the
# connection revoke the generation (sqlite3LookasideEnd). The bounds are the
# slot, lookaside.sz, which sqlite3DbMallocSize reports as usable. Q-15 rounds
# the lookaside buffer's sz*cnt up to its granule (tail pad only, counted).
#
# Not covered: the page cache. pcache1 recycles pages, and a bulk-local page
# goes back on its own free list without a free.
#
# Generated from sublet-3220000-memsys5.patch (the request-bounds port) by
# make-sublet-block-port.py beside this file.
